# SYNOPSIS

```text
/svc/swarm/file
/svc/swarm/ctl
/svc/swarm/status
/svc/swarm/nodes/<sys>
```

# DESCRIPTION

A swarm is the nodes listed in one file, the swarm file (ndb(6)), the same on every member: an entry each, with its `sys` name, its `ip`, its Noise IK public `key` and its `roles`.
Being in it is what lets a node in: 9gate(4) answers any member, and the cpu server (cpu(8)) any member whose `roles` have `terminal`.
The swarm service keeps that file on the node's volume, at `lib/ndb/swarm`, where cs reads it (ndb(8)), and is in the cpu images only, whose nodes make a swarm.
A cpu(8) session has its files at `/svc/swarm`, and 9gate's at `/svc/9gate`.

At its start it reads the file and finds this node's entry by the node's key, factotum's (factotum(4)), and starts what the entry's roles name.
For `file` it writes `listen tcp!*!564` <root> to 9gate's `ctl`, <root> the entry's `export` or `/fs`, fs(4)'s tree: the node's volume at `volume`, its snapshots at `snap`, and `send` and `receive`, through which members send each other their volumes' snapshots (send(1)).
`cpu` and `terminal` start nothing: the cpu server runs where the image has one.
A role added to this node's entry later is started when the file is next taken; one taken away stops nothing until the node restarts.

It listens on `tcp!*!17020`, sealed by Noise IK with the prologue `swarm` and the handshake run through factotum, and answers only a caller whose key the swarm file lists.
There it exports its `file` alone: another member's push is a write of it.

: `file`
  The swarm file.
  A write is the whole of a new one, which is taken only if each entry has one `sys`, without a `/`, and one `key` of 64 lowercase hex digits, and no two entries share a name or key; otherwise the write fails with `bad arg in system call`.
  A file taken is written to the volume, cs is told to reread it (`refresh` to `/net/cs`), `nodes` is made again, and this node's roles are applied.
: `ctl`
  Takes `push`: sends the file to every other member, one after another, at its `ip` on port 17020, as a write of its `file` there.
  It answers at once; `status` says how each went.
: `status`
  `node` <sys> `roles` <roles> for this node's entry, or `not a member`, or `no swarm file`; then a line for each role started (`file: asked 9gate to listen` ...); then a line for each member of the last push, `push` <sys> and `ok`, `skipped: no ip` or `failed:` and why.
: `nodes/`<sys>
  Each member's entry, as the file has it.

# EXAMPLES

From a cpu session on any member, add a node and send the new file to the rest:

```rc
cp /svc/swarm/file /data/swarm.new
echo 'sys=node3 key=7d0e… roles=cpu,file ip=192.168.0.31' >>/data/swarm.new
cat /data/swarm.new >/svc/swarm/file
echo push >/svc/swarm/ctl
cat /svc/swarm/status
```

`cp` can't write `file`: it creates its target, and the service's files can't be created.

